sn47.42:1.3 | S: What is the origin of the body? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is the origination of the body?
sn47.42:1.4 | S: The body originates from fuel. | T: With the origination of nutriment there is the origination of the body.
sn47.42:1.5 | S: When fuel ceases, the body disappears. | T: With the cessation of nutriment there is the passing away of the body.
sn47.42:1.6 | S: Feelings originate from contact. | T: “With the origination of contact there is the origination of feeling.
sn47.42:1.7 | S: When contact ceases, feelings disappear. | T: With the cessation of contact there is the passing away of feeling.
sn47.42:1.8 | S: The mind originates from name and form. | T: “With the origination of name-and-form there is the origination of mind.
sn47.42:1.9 | S: When name and form cease, the mind disappears. | T: With the cessation of name-and-form there is the passing away of mind.
sn47.42:1.10 | S: Principles originate from application of mind. | T: “With the origination of attention there is the origination of phenomena.
sn47.42:1.11 | S: When application of mind disappears, principles disappear.” | T: With the cessation of attention there is the passing away of phenomena.”
sn47.42:1.12 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn47.44
sn47.44:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, a mendicant should live mindfully. | T: “Bhikkhus, a bhikkhu should dwell mindful.
sn47.44:1.2 | S: This is my instruction to you. | T: This is our instruction to you.
sn47.44:1.3 | S: And how is a mendicant mindful? | T: “And how, bhikkhus, is a bhikkhu mindful?
sn47.44:1.4 | S: It’s when a mendicant meditates by observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu dwells contemplating the body in the body, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.44:1.5 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings … | T: He dwells contemplating feelings in feelings …
sn47.44:1.6 | S: mind … | T: mind in mind …
sn47.44:1.7 | S: principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: phenomena in phenomena, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.44:1.8 | S: That’s how a mendicant is mindful. | T: It is in this way, bhikkhus, that a bhikkhu is mindful.
sn47.44:1.9 | S: A mendicant should live mindfully. | T: “Bhikkhus, a bhikkhu should dwell mindful.
sn47.44:1.10 | S: This is my instruction to you.” | T: This is our instruction to you.”
sn47.44:1.11 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

