## sn47.47
sn47.47:1.1 | S: Then a mendicant went up to the Buddha … and said: | T: Then a certain bhikkhu approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side, and said to him:
sn47.47:1.2 | S: “Sir, may the Buddha please teach me Dhamma in brief. When I’ve heard it, I’ll live alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute.” | T: “Venerable sir, it would be good if the Blessed One would teach me the Dhamma in brief, so that, having heard the Dhamma from the Blessed One, I might dwell alone, withdrawn, diligent, ardent, and resolute.”
sn47.47:1.3 | S: “Well then, mendicant, you should purify the starting point of skillful qualities. | T: “In that case, bhikkhu, purify the very beginning of wholesome states.
sn47.47:1.4 | S: What is the beginning of skillful qualities? | T: And what is the beginning of wholesome states?
sn47.47:1.5 | S: Give up bad conduct by way of body, speech, and mind and develop good conduct by way of body, speech, and mind. | T: Here, bhikkhu, having abandoned bodily misconduct, you should develop good bodily conduct.
sn47.47:1.6 | P: Vacīduccaritaṁ pahāya vacīsucaritaṁ bhāvessasi. | T: Having abandoned verbal misconduct, you should develop good verbal conduct.
sn47.47:1.7 | P: Manoduccaritaṁ pahāya manosucaritaṁ bhāvessasi. | T: Having abandoned mental misconduct, you should develop good mental conduct.
sn47.47:1.8 | S: When you’ve done this, you should develop the four kinds of mindfulness meditation, depending on and grounded on ethics. | T: When, bhikkhu, having abandoned bodily misconduct … you have developed good mental conduct, then, based upon virtue, established upon virtue, you should develop the four establishments of mindfulness.
sn47.47:2.1 | S: What four? | T: “What four?
sn47.47:2.2 | S: Meditate observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: Here, bhikkhu, a bhikkhu dwells contemplating the body in the body …
sn47.47:2.3 | S: Meditate observing an aspect of feelings … | T: feelings in feelings …
sn47.47:2.4 | S: mind … | T: mind in mind …
sn47.47:2.5 | S: principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: phenomena in phenomena, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.47:2.6 | S: When you develop the four kinds of mindfulness meditation in this way, depending on and grounded on ethics, you can expect growth, not decline, in skillful qualities, whether by day or by night.” … | T: “When, bhikkhu, based upon virtue, established upon virtue, you develop these four establishments of mindfulness in such a way, then, whether night or day comes, you may expect only growth in wholesome states, not decline.”
sn47.47:2.7 | S: And that mendicant became one of the perfected. | T: Then that bhikkhu … became one of the arahants.
sn47.47:2.8 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

