## sn47.4
sn47.4:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Kosalans near the brahmin village of Sālā. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling among the Kosalans at the brahmin village of Sala.
sn47.4:1.2 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants: | T: There the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn47.4:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, those mendicants who are junior—recently gone forth, newly come to this teaching and training—should be encouraged, supported, and established by you in the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. | T: “Bhikkhus, those bhikkhus who are newly ordained, not long gone forth, recently come to this Dhamma and Discipline, should be exhorted, settled, and established by you in the development of the four establishments of mindfulness.
sn47.4:2.2 | S: What four? | T: What four?
sn47.4:2.3 | S: Please, reverends, meditate observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, at one, with minds that are clear, immersed and unified in samādhi, so as to truly know the body. | T: “‘Come, friends, dwell contemplating the body in the body, ardent, clearly comprehending, unified, with limpid mind, concentrated, with one-pointed mind, in order to know the body as it really is.
sn47.4:2.4 | S: Meditate observing an aspect of feelings—keen, aware, at one, with minds that are clear, immersed and unified in samādhi, so as to truly know feelings. | T: Dwell contemplating feelings in feelings … in order to know feelings as they really are.
sn47.4:2.5 | S: Meditate observing an aspect of the mind—keen, aware, at one, with minds that are clear, immersed and unified in samādhi, so as to truly know the mind. | T: Dwell contemplating mind in mind … in order to know mind as it really is.
sn47.4:2.6 | S: Meditate observing an aspect of principles—keen, aware, at one, with minds that are clear, immersed and unified in samādhi, so as to truly know principles. | T: Dwell contemplating phenomena in phenomena … in order to know phenomena as they really are.’
sn47.4:3.1 | S: Those mendicants who are trainees—who haven’t achieved their heart’s desire, but live aspiring to the supreme sanctuary from the yoke—also meditate observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, at one, with minds that are clear, immersed and unified in samādhi, so as to fully understand the body. | T: “Bhikkhus, those bhikkhus who are trainees, who have not attained their mind’s ideal, who dwell aspiring for the unsurpassed security from bondage: they too dwell contemplating the body in the body, ardent, clearly comprehending, unified, with limpid mind, concentrated, with one-pointed mind, in order to fully understand the body as it really is.
sn47.4:3.2 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings—keen, aware, at one, with minds that are clear, immersed and unified in samādhi, so as to fully understand feelings. | T: They too dwell contemplating feelings in feelings … in order to fully understand feelings as they really are.
sn47.4:3.3 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of the mind—keen, aware, at one, with minds that are clear, immersed and unified in samādhi, so as to fully understand the mind. | T: They too dwell contemplating mind in mind … in order to fully understand mind as it really is.
sn47.4:3.4 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of principles—keen, aware, at one, with minds that are clear, immersed and unified in samādhi, so as to fully understand principles. | T: They too dwell contemplating phenomena in phenomena … in order to fully understand phenomena as they really are.
sn47.4:4.1 | S: Those mendicants who are perfected—who have ended the defilements, completed the spiritual journey, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, achieved their own goal, utterly ended the fetter of continued existence, and are rightly freed through enlightenment—also meditate observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, at one, with minds that are clear, immersed and unified in samādhi, detached from the body. | T: “Bhikkhus, those bhikkhus who are arahants, whose taints are destroyed, who have lived the holy life, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, reached their own goal, utterly destroyed the fetters of existence, and are completely liberated through final knowledge: they too dwell contemplating the body in the body, ardent, clearly comprehending, unified, with limpid mind, concentrated, with one-pointed mind, detached from the body.
sn47.4:4.2 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings—keen, aware, at one, with minds that are clear, immersed and unified in samādhi, detached from feelings. | T: They too dwell contemplating feelings in feelings … detached from feelings.
sn47.4:4.3 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of the mind—keen, aware, at one, with minds that are clear, immersed and unified in samādhi, detached from the mind. | T: They too dwell contemplating mind in mind … detached from mind.
sn47.4:4.4 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of principles—keen, aware, at one, with minds that are clear, immersed and unified in samādhi, detached from principles. | T: They too dwell contemplating phenomena in phenomena … detached from phenomena.
sn47.4:5.1 | S: Those mendicants who are junior—recently gone forth, newly come to this teaching and training—should be encouraged, supported, and established by you in these four kinds of mindfulness meditation.” | T: “Bhikkhus, those bhikkhus who are newly ordained, not long gone forth, recently come to this Dhamma and Discipline, should be exhorted, settled, and established by you in the development of these four establishments of mindfulness.”
sn47.4:5.2 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn47.50
