## sn47.51-62
sn47.51-62:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, the Ganges River slants, slopes, and inclines to the east. | T: “Bhikkhus, just as the river Ganges slants, slopes, and inclines towards the east,
sn47.51-62:1.2 | S: In the same way, a mendicant who develops and cultivates the four kinds of mindfulness meditation slants, slopes, and inclines to extinguishment. | T: so too a bhikkhu who develops and cultivates the four establishments of mindfulness slants, slopes, and inclines towards Nibbāna.
sn47.51-62:2.1 | S: And how does a mendicant who develops the four kinds of mindfulness meditation slant, slope, and incline to extinguishment? | T: “And how, bhikkhus, does a bhikkhu develop and cultivate the four establishments of mindfulness so that he slants, slopes, and inclines towards Nibbāna?
sn47.51-62:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant meditates by observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu dwells contemplating the body in the body …
sn47.51-62:2.3 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings … | T: feelings in feelings …
sn47.51-62:2.4 | S: mind … | T: mind in mind …
sn47.51-62:2.5 | S: principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: phenomena in phenomena, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.51-62:2.6 | S: That’s how a mendicant who develops and cultivates the four kinds of mindfulness meditation slants, slopes, and inclines to extinguishment.” | T: It is in this way, bhikkhus, that a bhikkhu develops and cultivates the four establishments of mindfulness so that he slants, slopes, and inclines towards Nibbāna.”
sn47.51-62:2.7 | P: Gaṅgāpeyyālavaggo chaṭṭho. | T: (The remaining suttas of this vagga are to be similarly elaborated parallel to SN45.92–95.)
sn47.51-62:3.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
sn47.51-62:3.1 | S: Six on slanting to the east, | T: Six about slanting to the east
sn47.51-62:3.2 | S: and six on slanting to the ocean; | T: And six about slanting to the ocean.
sn47.51-62:3.3 | S: these two sixes make twelve, | T: These two sixes make up twelve:
sn47.51-62:3.4 | S: and that’s how this chapter is recited. | T: Thus the subchapter is recited.

## sn47.5
sn47.5:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn47.5:1.2 | S: There the Buddha said: | T: There the Blessed One said this:
sn47.5:1.3 | S: “Rightly speaking, mendicants, you’d call the five hindrances a ‘heap of the unskillful’. | T: “Bhikkhus, if one were to say of anything ‘a heap of the unwholesome,’ it is about the five hindrances that one could rightly say this.
sn47.5:1.4 | S: For these five hindrances are entirely a heap of the unskillful. | T: For this is a complete heap of the unwholesome, that is, the five hindrances.
sn47.5:1.5 | S: What five? | T: What five?
sn47.5:1.6 | S: The hindrances of sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt. | T: The hindrance of sensual desire, the hindrance of ill will, the hindrance of sloth and torpor, the hindrance of restlessness and remorse, the hindrance of doubt.
sn47.5:1.7 | S: Rightly speaking, you’d call these five hindrances a ‘heap of the unskillful’. | T: If one were to say of anything ‘a heap of the unwholesome,’ it is about these five hindrances that one could rightly say this.
sn47.5:1.8 | S: For these five hindrances are entirely a heap of the unskillful. | T: For this is a complete heap of the unwholesome, that is, the five hindrances.
sn47.5:2.1 | S: Rightly speaking, you’d call these four kinds of mindfulness meditation a ‘heap of the skillful’. | T: “If, bhikkhus, one were to say of anything ‘a heap of the wholesome, ’ it is about the four establishments of mindfulness that one could rightly say this.
sn47.5:2.2 | S: For these four kinds of mindfulness meditation are entirely a heap of the skillful. | T: For this is a complete heap of the wholesome, that is, the four establishments of mindfulness.
sn47.5:2.3 | S: What four? | T: What four?
sn47.5:2.4 | S: It’s when a mendicant meditates by observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu dwells contemplating the body in the body, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.5:2.5 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings … | T: He dwells contemplating feelings in feelings …
sn47.5:2.6 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of the mind … | T: mind in mind …
sn47.5:2.7 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: phenomena in phenomena, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.5:2.8 | S: Rightly speaking, you’d call these four kinds of mindfulness meditation a ‘heap of the skillful’. | T: If one were to say of anything ‘a heap of the wholesome, ’ it is about these four establishments of mindfulness that one could rightly say this.
sn47.5:2.9 | S: For these four kinds of mindfulness meditation are entirely a heap of the skillful.” | T: For this is a complete heap of the wholesome, that is, the four establishments of mindfulness.”
