## sn47.6
sn47.6:1.1 | S: “Once upon a time, mendicants, a hawk suddenly swooped down and grabbed a quail. | T: “Bhikkhus, once in the past a hawk suddenly swooped down and seized a quail.
sn47.6:1.2 | S: And as the quail was being carried off he wailed, | T: Then, while the quail was being carried off by the hawk, he lamented:
sn47.6:1.3 | S: ‘I’m so unlucky, of so little merit, to have roamed out of my territory into the domain of others. | T: ‘We were so unlucky, of so little merit! We strayed out of our own resort into the domain of others.
sn47.6:1.4 | S: If today I’d roamed within my own territory, the domain of my fathers, this hawk wouldn’t have stood a chance against me in a fight.’ | T: If we had stayed in our own resort today, in our own ancestral domain, this hawk wouldn’t have stood a chance against me in a fight.’—
sn47.6:1.5 | S: ‘So, quail, what is your own territory, the domain of your fathers?’ | T: ‘But what is your own resort, quail, what is your own ancestral domain?’—
sn47.6:1.6 | S: ‘It’s a ploughed field covered with clods of earth.’ | T: ‘The freshly ploughed field covered with clods of soil.’
sn47.6:1.7 | S: Confident in her own strength, the hawk was not daunted or intimidated. She released the quail, saying, | T: “Then the hawk, confident of her own strength, not boasting of her own strength, released the quail, saying:
sn47.6:1.8 | S: ‘Go now, quail. But even there you won’t escape me!’ | T: ‘Go now, quail, but even there you won’t escape me.’
sn47.6:2.1 | S: Then the quail went to a ploughed field covered with clods of earth. He climbed up a big clod, and standing there, he said to the hawk: | T: “Then, bhikkhus, the quail went to a freshly ploughed field covered with clods of soil. Having climbed up on a large clod, he stood there and addressed the hawk:
sn47.6:2.2 | S: ‘Come get me, hawk! Come get me, hawk!’ | T: ‘Come get me now, hawk! Come get me now, hawk!’
sn47.6:2.3 | S: Confident in her own strength, the hawk was not daunted or intimidated. She folded her wings and suddenly swooped down on the quail. | T: “Then the hawk, confident of her own strength, not boasting of her own strength, folded up both her wings and suddenly swooped down on the quail.
sn47.6:2.4 | S: When the quail knew that the hawk was nearly there, he slipped under that clod. | T: But when the quail knew, ‘That hawk has come close,’ he slipped inside that clod,
sn47.6:2.5 | S: But the hawk crashed chest-first right there. | T: and the hawk shattered her breast right on the spot.
sn47.6:2.6 | S: That’s what happens when you roam out of your territory into the domain of others. | T: So it is, bhikkhus, when one strays outside one’s own resort into the domain of others.
sn47.6:3.1 | S: So, mendicants, don’t roam out of your own territory into the domain of others. | T: “Therefore, bhikkhus, do not stray outside your own resort into the domain of others.
sn47.6:3.2 | S: If you roam out of your own territory into the domain of others, Māra will find a vulnerability and get an opportunity. | T: Mara will gain access to those who stray outside their own resort into the domain of others; Mara will get a hold on them.
sn47.6:3.3 | S: And what is not a mendicant’s own territory but the domain of others? | T: “And what is not a bhikkhu’s own resort but the domain of others?
sn47.6:3.4 | S: It’s the five kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: It is the five cords of sensual pleasure.
sn47.6:3.5 | S: What five? | T: What five?
sn47.6:3.6 | S: Sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: Forms cognizable by the eye that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasing, sensually enticing, tantalizing.
sn47.6:3.7 | S: Sounds known by the ear … | T: Sounds cognizable by the ear …
sn47.6:3.8 | S: Smells known by the nose … | T: Odours cognizable by the nose …
sn47.6:3.9 | S: Tastes known by the tongue … | T: Tastes cognizable by the tongue …
sn47.6:3.10 | S: Touches known by the body, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: Tactile objects cognizable by the body that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasing, sensually enticing, tantalizing. These are the five cords of sensual pleasure.
sn47.6:3.11 | S: This is not a mendicant’s own territory but the domain of others. | T: This is what is not a bhikkhu’s own resort but the domain of others.
sn47.6:4.1 | S: You should roam inside your own territory, the domain of your fathers. | T: “Move in your own resort, bhikkhus, in your own ancestral domain.
sn47.6:4.2 | S: If you roam inside your own territory, the domain of your fathers, Māra won’t find a vulnerability or get an opportunity. | T: Mara will not gain access to those who move in their own resort, in their own ancestral domain; Mara will not get a hold on them.
sn47.6:4.3 | S: And what is a mendicant’s own territory, the domain of the fathers? | T: “And what is a bhikkhu’s resort, his own ancestral domain?
sn47.6:4.4 | P: Yadidaṁ— | T: (none)
sn47.6:4.5 | S: It’s the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. | T: It is the four establishments of mindfulness.
sn47.6:4.6 | S: What four? | T: What four?
sn47.6:4.7 | S: It’s when a mendicant meditates by observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu dwells contemplating the body in the body, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.6:4.8 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings … | T: He dwells contemplating feelings in feelings …
sn47.6:4.9 | S: mind … | T: mind in mind …
sn47.6:4.10 | S: principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: phenomena in phenomena, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.6:4.11 | S: This is a mendicant’s own territory, the domain of the fathers.” | T: This is a bhikkhu’s resort, his own ancestral domain.”
sn47.6:4.12 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)


